0017-debugger-ack-timeout.patch
# q5-keystore: the AppleKeyStore service in software (keystore/: UniXNU's AppleKeyStoreState, class keys, key wrap) is
# started from the board's late start; owner-dataprotect (a file's protection class kept in an extended attribute) is
# on by default. Not a security boundary: keystore/README.md.
0022-keystore.patch
# q4a-framebuffer (Q4a): the display. The graphics family is loaded and the framebuffer backend (display/: UniXNU's
# NativeMobileDisplay under the collection's IOMobileFramebufferLegacy, scanned out through the virtio GPU) is started
# without boot arguments (oneios-display=0 turns it off); the boot framebuffer, memory of the host's RAM object above
# the kernel's window, may be mapped at a kernel address the way IOKit maps device memory.
0020-display.patch
# lead: the three messages of the return-to-user path (task process gone, twin not made, thread_run refused) are
# printed with interrupts on: the serial console panicked on them ("Console I/O from interrupt-disabled context").
0023-user-return-console.patch
# native-adreno: the GPU's pool (the host RAM object's pages between the kernel's window and the boot framebuffer,
# /arm-io/virtio-gpu drm-host-pool) may be mapped into a task and at a kernel address, as 0020 allows the boot
# framebuffer: the virtio-gpu 3D driver maps each context's response buffer from it. No patch links NativeAdreno:
# Metal's kernel side (the AGXAccelerator under the collection's IOAcceleratorES, AGXDeviceUserClient) is in the GPU
# archive of 0005 (gpu/build-oneios-gpu.py, gpu/OneiOSAdreno.cpp) and starts from oneios_gpu3d_start.
0024-gpu-pool.patch
# fable-user (Q6): the host's text fixups on code pages only, once, with a record of the changed words so that a
# page validated again is hashed as it was read ("Text page corruption detected" otherwise); a twin that cannot be
# made is tried again before the panic; hosted_mapcheck=1 (a debugging record of every task's host mappings).
# 0025-README.md. Goes with xnuhost's task.c of the same commit (one task creation at a time, waits by the clock).
0025-text-fixup-once.patch
# lead: a debugging aid. oneios-ubt=NAME,... prints, oneios-ubt-at seconds in, where every thread of those user
# processes is (saved pc, lr, and the return addresses up its frame pointers). Off without the boot argument.
0026-user-backtrace.patch
# lead: on a host whose user addresses are narrower than 47 bits (the S24 Ultra: 39) iOS user space runs without
# pointer authentication: the codes would sit in bits iOS code masks as address bits (libobjc's isa mask keeps
# bits 3..42; newfs_apfs died in objc's map_images at the first phone boot). The kernel signs nothing for user
# space and every task thread runs with its keys off. oneios-user-pac=0|1 forces it.
0027-user-pac-off-narrow-host.patch
# lead: the user exception dump (sheng-exc-name) also prints the text the registers point at: the message of a
# process that stops itself on an assertion (SpringBoard in FrontBoardServices on the phone), when no crash report
# is written.
0028-exception-text.patch
# lead: a debugging aid. oneios-iouc=NAME,... logs which IOKit user client methods those processes call and what
# they return (first call of each class and selector, and each change of status). Off without the boot argument.
0029-iokit-client-trace.patch
# lead: a debugging aid. oneios-display-report=SECONDS prints the display backend's counters (vsync ticks, swaps
# validated and refused, transfers, retirements, the family's state words). Off without the boot argument.
0030-display-report.patch
# native-adreno: the Metal plug-in reaches iOS. UniXNU's addon mount is on without its boot argument (sheng-gpu-addon=0
# turns it off) and takes the Data seed's folder (/private/var/oneios/addons/Extensions) unless a GPU addon of this boot
# (kernel.kc.gpuaddon) was written to /private/var/ngpu; its result and every open of an AGX service are logged.
0031-gpu-addon-mount.patch
# amfi: the App cryptex volume (read-only, on the host-verified block device) answers FSIOC_AUTH_FS, the
# on-authorized-authapfs-volume fact of AMFI's launch constraints: its daemons have constraint category 1 or 2 in the
# trust cache and crash-looped with "c[1]p[1]m[1]e[1]". No other volume changes, no other launch-constraint fact changes.
0032-app-cryptex-authenticated.patch
# native-adreno: the accelerator's grant (NativeSandboxGrantProcess) decides the open for the processes it names even with
# the class chains adopted: the iPhone's profiles refused iconservicesagent (no icons on the home screen).
0033-gpu-grant-decides.patch
# lead, a debugging aid (off without its boot argument): oneios-sysfail=NAME,... prints the file system calls of
# those processes that fail, with the path and the errno. The guest's user-space log is not on the console.
0034-syscall-failures.patch
# lead: the removable system apps (Camera, Photos, Safari, ...: the OS volume's /private/var/staged_system_apps) are
# shown over /Applications unless owner-staged-apps=0. UniXNU's view wanted owner-staged-apps=1, which xnuhost never
# passes: the phone's Home Screen and Dock were empty.
0035-staged-apps-on.patch
# lead: iconservicesagent's drawn icons are dropped once when the store's generation is not this kernel's (icons drawn
# while the GPU open was refused or no apps were registered stayed blank: the store is keyed by build and language).
0036-icon-store-generation.patch
# lead: lsd's first-boot semaphore is made before launchd (Droid-VM's mark_lsd_first_boot_done): without it lsd drops
# the apps of the staged-apps view from the second boot of a Data store on.
0037-lsd-first-boot.patch
# mac-kernel: proc_pid() declared in hosted_machine.c (0009/0012's thread dump): vm/vm_protos.h declares it only for
# DEVELOPMENT || DEBUG, so the RELEASE reference of the prelink (XNU_FILESET_REFERENCE=1) failed on the Mac (Xcode's
# clang: implicit declaration is an error; the Linux wrapper's -w hid it). The file is the hosted layer's own (no guard).
0038-proc-pid-declared.patch
# gpu: one list of the processes that get a Metal device for both gates. The kernel writes the plug-in's
# NativeAdrenoMetal.clients beside the bundle at every boot from the list the grant decides by (droidvm-gpu-clients or the
# GPU archive's default, OneiOSAdreno.cpp; oneios-gpu-plugin-clients for the plug-in alone), so a new list needs no new
# Data seed. The plug-in's text rows (open types 0x414770cc) are one oneios-gpu-text line a row, not a line a character.
0039-gpu-client-list-and-texts.patch
# perf: the idle cache flush (cpu_idle -> platform_cache_idle_enter -> FlushPoU_Dcache) does nothing: a vCPU is a
# Linux thread that never powers a core down, and each set/way `dc` and cache-geometry access around it is a trapped
# instruction xnuhost emulates by a signal (6 per idle entry with setway-skip, about 600 entries a second when idle).
0040-idle-cache-flush-off.patch
# perf: oneios-perf=SECONDS (default 30, 0 = off), the kernel's share of user traps on the console: syscalls by kind
# and by number (calls and mean vCPU time from the trap to the next user entry or idle), faults filled from the
# tables or sent to vm_fault, the pmap's maps and unmaps, the TLB lock's contention and hold time. Counters only.
0041-perf-report.patch
# perf: a debugging aid for configd's IOKit-quiet wait (en0 named minutes late): at 30, 60, 120, 240, 480 s the busy
# services with no busy child and the count (oneios-iobusy=0 turns it off).
0042-iokit-busy-report.patch
# perf: lsd's database drop at every boot (native_ls_store_drop) is kept when the database already names every app of
# /Applications (oneios-ls-keep=2, default; 1 keep, 0 drop as before): lsd then does not register every app again
# (6-11 minutes on the phone, while chronod and SpringBoard's main thread wait for it).
0043-ls-store-keep.patch
# perf: jobs the Data store's disabled.plist disables but that must run are enabled again before launchd
# (oneios-enable-jobs=, default com.apple.analyticsd: without it CoreAnalytics' clients retry in a loop; backboardd 217%).
0044-enable-jobs.patch
# perf: a TLB lock per user pmap (the kernel pmap keeps the global one): a fill, flush or copy of one task no longer
# waits for another's (the global lock was waited for up to 1.5 vCPUs on the phone); a flush of the shared region goes
# only to the tasks that filled pages in its 32 MiB blocks; oneios-perf says where the unmaps come from.
0045-tlb-lock-per-pmap.patch
# perf: oneios-fault-around=N (off by default): a fill also maps the other pages of its N-page window that the tables
# already allow, as a TLB may.
0046-fault-around.patch
